fix: only declare the entries and addresses a version really has

The client run of 1.4.4 failed with 'package(bedrockdata): version(1.21.70.04-client.1)
not found!': tags up to 1.7.7 hardcode 'local is_client = false' (the target_type
branch is commented out) and their client-only dependencies were never published,
and 0.8/0.13 have no target_type option at all, so asking for the client flavour
either dies while resolving or silently builds the server one. The ll plugin now
declares the client entry only for a tag that has the option and honours it
(1.8 onwards).

LeviLamina also only started building itself with clang-cl at 26.20. Older tags
are msvc builds and no clang-cl plugin project exists for them, so publishing a
clang-cl address for those would hand a project a build its own version never
had. The clang-cl alias is therefore declared only where the upstream file really
forces clang-cl; the msvc alias stays (a project that writes
set_toolchains("msvc") still needs it). The config-driven plugins get the same
filter: an address survives only if it is the toolchain that compiled the sources
or the one the package is resolved with.

Because the declared profiles are compared with what an entry records, the
already published 1.4.4 server entry is queued again -- which is what removes the
clang-cl address it should never have had.
